Moeve (formerly CEPSA, founded 1929) is a Spanish energy group with 10,890 employees in 22 countries. It refines crude oil, trades energy, sells fuels and makes petrochemicals

Energy Parks: two of Spain's largest refineries, at San Roque and Palos de la Frontera
– Produced 20.1Mt in 2025, running at 90% of capacity and earning $7.9 per barrel (2024: $7.0); more than €2.5bn invested in the sites over the last five and a half years

Commercial: sold 18.2Mt of fuels and products in 2025 (+6%) to airlines, shipping, wholesale and lubricants customers, and through Spain's second-largest service station network – 2,055 sites, including c.400 low-cost Ballenoil stations

Chemicals: world leader in LAB (a detergent ingredient) and #2 in Europe in phenol / acetone; management called 2025 EBITDA of €181m the bottom of the cycle, and earnings are recovering in 2026 (H1: €172m)

Positive Motion Strategy (2022–30): shifting the business towards sustainable mobility and green fuels, with new investments required to return at least 15%
– A record 55% of 2025 capex (c.70% in H1-26) went to the energy transition, mainly a 300MW green hydrogen plant (Onuba: approved Feb-26, >€1bn, 51% owned, start-up 2029) and a second-generation biofuels plant in Huelva (over half built)

Exit from oil and gas production: c.70% sold since 2022 – Abu Dhabi (2023), Colombia and Peru (2024) and Suriname (2025). In Nov-26, Moeve agreed to sell its remaining Algerian assets (c.30kbopd) to Pertamina for a reported c.€1bn

2025 results: revenue of €23,381m (-6%) and EBITDA of €1,685m (-9%; c.7% margin), held back by the April Iberian blackout (cost >€50m); operations generated €1.5bn of cash
– Net debt of €2.4bn (1.6x EBITDA) and €5.5bn of liquidity, enough to cover all debt due before 2030; investment grade at Moody's and Fitch; dividends reduced to €108m in 2025

2026 trading: EBITDA for the first nine months roughly doubled to €2,409m, driven by strong refining margins ($11.2/bbl in H1); leverage fell to 1.2x by June

Galp and retail: the downstream merger with Galp, announced in Jan-26, was called off on 26-Oct-26 after signing had slipped from mid-2026 to H2-26; Rothschild has since been hired to explore a sale of the c.1,700-site retail network
